	affiliations = {Christian Doppler Lab. MEVSS, Johannes Kepler University Linz, Linz, Austria; Computer Science and Engineering, University of Notre Dame, Notre Dame, IN, United States; Institute for Software Systems Engineering, Johannes Kepler University Linz, Linz, Austria},
	abstract = {Complex software-intensive systems are often described as systems of systems (SoS) due to their heterogeneous architectural elements. As SoS behavior is often only understandable during operation, runtime monitoring is needed to detect deviations from requirements. Today, while diverse monitoring approaches exist, most do not provide what is needed to monitor SoS, e.g., support for dynamically defining and deploying diverse checks across multiple systems. In this paper we report on our experiences of developing, applying, and evolving an approach for monitoring an SoS in the domain of industrial automation software, that is based on a domain-specific language (DSL). We first describe our initial approach to dynamically define and check constraints in SoS at runtime and then motivate and describe its evolution based on requirements elicited in an industry collaboration project. We furthermore describe solutions we have developed to support the evolution of our approach, i.e., a code generation approach and a framework to automate testing the DSL after changes. We evaluate the expressiveness and scalability of our new DSL-based approach using an industrial SoS. We also discuss lessons we learned. Our results show that while developing a DSL-based approach is a good solution to support industrial users, one must prepare the approach for evolution, by making it extensible and adaptable to future scenarios. Particularly, support for automated (re-)generation of tools and code after changes and automated testing are essential. © 2018, Springer Science+Business Media, LLC, part of Springer Nature.},
	author_keywords = {NEW, v4_search, source_scopus, Constraint checking, Domain-specific languages, DSL evolution, Requirements monitoring, Systems of systems},
	keywords = {Automation; Digital subscriber lines; Graphical user interfaces; Problem oriented languages; System of systems; Systems engineering; Architectural element; Constraint checking; Domain specific language (DSL); Domain specific languages; Industrial automation; Industry collaboration; Requirements monitoring; Systems of systems; Monitoring},
