	abstract = {Monocular Depth Estimation (MDE) is a fundamental task in 3D scene understanding and has witnessed significant advancements with the rise of encoder-decoder architectures. Although visual-only methods have made significant progress in MDE by relying on visual cues, they still struggle with ambiguities in occluded regions, textureless surfaces, and scale ambiguity. To mitigate these issues, vision-language methods incorporate textual semantic priors to improve absolute scale reasoning and spatial understanding, yet they often lack fine-grained cross-modal alignment, leading to suboptimal semantic-geometry fusion. To address these challenges, we propose a text-guided multi-scale fusion framework (TFDepth) for MDE. It mainly consists of a Cross-modality Semantic Enhancement Module (CSEM) and a Laplacian Pyramid Residual Fusion Module (LPRFM). For CSEM, it leverages Multi-head Cross-Attention (MHCA) to integrate high-level textual information with visual features. This mechanism directs the model toward semantically relevant regions across different scales to selectively enhance meaningful structures and suppress irrelevant signals. Furthermore, LPRFM captures structural residuals at multiple levels to refine object boundaries and recover fine geometric details. Experiments on both indoor (NYU Depth V2) and outdoor (KITTI) datasets demonstrate that TFDepth consistently outperforms existing approaches. The source code is available at https://github.com/YOUNY3/TFDepth. © 2026},
